{
  "dom_id": "step-q211376",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Expressed as a mixed number in its lowest terms, $2\\,\\frac{3}{4}\\times 6=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $2\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+3}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $6$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n6=\\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{4}\\times \\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($6$) have a common factor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{4}\\times \\frac{6}{1} & =\\frac{11}{1}\\times \\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =11\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\times 3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{33}{2}=16\\,\\text{R}\\,1=16\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$",
